A binding gives a value a visible name.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count = 42
    ratio F64 = 0.5
]

### Declaration forms

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    name = value
    name Type = value
]

- A binding must have an initializer.
- Without an annotation, the initializer supplies its shape and numeric-literal family. A retained literal origin can wait for eligible exact receivers in the same body before defaulting.
- An explicit annotation supplies an exact receiving type. Raw literals can materialise directly in it, while already typed values need exact compatibility.
- Ordinary bindings are immutable by default.
- `=` creates a binding when the name is new.
- An existing mutable place requires `~` before the whole target for a direct or compound write, as in `~count = 1` or `~point.x += 1`.

### Numeric inference in a runtime body

Numeric inference stays within one runtime body, including its nested blocks,
branches, loops and handlers. Only retained literal origins and the permitted
receiving relationships participate. Copies and writes can connect the same
origin, but a later use cannot invent a missing shape or infer named arithmetic
operands backwards.

This does not add general later-use inference or change a completed value's
type. @../numbers/#numeric-literals (Numeric literals) owns the receiving
contexts, source-order-independent constraints and fixed `I32`/`F64` defaults.

Compile-time constants use `#` and are documented separately by @../constants (Constants and compile-time behaviour).

### Binding mode and type

Binding mode is not semantic type identity.

An immutable `String` binding and a mutable `String` binding still have the
same semantic `String` type. The binding decides which later operations are
permitted.

### Scope

A binding is visible in its scope and child scopes unless a language rule
creates a narrower visibility boundary.

Moth does not allow a visible name to be redeclared. See the Shadowing
section for the exact rule.
